# 6 Common pitfalls and how to diagnose a run {#ch:pitfalls}

These are the problems that most often waste a day. Most were met in real
projects; the first two were found while preparing the examples of this
guide.

## The program

### The run stops after one step

No event has an upper limit, so `run()` has nothing left to wait for
([section 2.5](02_language#sec:events)). Give one event an end: `event end (t = 10) {}` is
enough.

### An output time is skipped

`t += 0.1; t <= 0.3` misses $t = 0.3$ because of rounding. Use
`t <= 0.3 + 1e-6` or a list of times.

### `initializer element is not constant`

Global variables in C can only be initialised with constants. Declare
`double c = 0.;` at file scope and compute `c = f(...);` in
`main()` before `run()`.

### Dimension errors for correct code

The static dimension check of `qcc` reports false errors, most often
for `cond ? expression : 0.`. Compile with `-disable-dimensions`
once you are sure the code is right.

### Results change with the number of threads

A loop that accumulates into a variable has no `reduction()` clause.

## Boundary conditions

### Open boundaries

Write velocity and pressure conditions as pairs ([table 18.1](18_boundary)), and
give `pf` the same condition as `p`. A domain where every side
prescribes the normal velocity needs the inflow and outflow to balance
exactly; a closed box with an inflow has no pressure solution, and the
multigrid solver then fails to converge or crawls.

### Imposing a volume fraction

Never write `f[left] = dirichlet(cond ? 1 : 0)`. The ghost value is
$2f_b - f_i$, between $-1$ and 2 wherever the computed interface is not
exactly at the target, and the interfacial force (gravity with
`reduced.h`, surface tension) turns that jump into a large force on the
first interior face. Use `neumann(0)` or set the ghost value to a
volume fraction directly ([section 18.8](18_boundary#sec:bc-f)).

### Inflow profiles in two-phase flows

An analytic inflow velocity (a wave, a jet) is usually valid in one fluid
only. Applied over the whole boundary, it can drive the other fluid many
times faster than the physical flow and collapse the time step. Multiply the
profile by the volume fraction of the fluid it describes.

### Wave makers and absorbing layers

A velocity inlet reflects everything that comes back to it, and a pressure
outlet reflects unless the flow arriving there is calm. Use a relaxation
zone at the inlet and a damping layer at the outlet ([chapter 20](20_zones)), and
measure the reflection: a layer of about one wavelength is needed for
reflection of a few percent, and a stronger damping in a short layer
reflects more because the damping itself becomes an obstacle.

## Embedded solids

### Tiny cut cells

A cell that is almost all solid ($c_s \sim 10^{-4}$) has a tiny volume, and
a pressure difference across it gives a huge velocity. This happens most
where an interface meets the solid. Remove such cells after every call to
`solid()`:

~~~literatec
solid (cs, fs, phi);
foreach()
  if (cs[] < 1e-2)
    cs[] = 0.;
fractions_cleanup (cs, fs);
~~~

Do this in one function and call it at initialisation, after a restore and
after each adaptation.

### Restarting with a solid

`dump()` does not save face fields, so `fs` is missing after
`restore()`. Rebuild the solid before the first step.

### Forces with reduced gravity

With `reduced.h`, `p` is the dynamic pressure. Add the hydrostatic
part before computing forces, and check sign and size with a submerged
body at rest, which must feel its buoyancy.

## Domain and resolution

### The domain is a cube

On trees, `size()` sets one length for all directions. Making the
domain longer makes it taller and wider too. Define positions and lengths
from physical quantities (a wavelength, a diameter), and compute `L0`
from them, never the other way round; a position written as a fraction of
`L0` moves silently when the domain changes.

### Points on cell faces

A gauge exactly on a cell face or on the domain boundary can return a
neighbour's value or `nodata`. Offset probe points slightly.

### Disk space

A dump of a 3D run at level 8 or more can take tens of megabytes. A dump
event every few time units fills a disk quota quickly.

## Diagnosing a collapsing time step

When `dt` shrinks step after step, some velocity in the domain is far
too large. Find it:

~~~literatec
event check (i++)
{
  double umax = 0.; coord pmax = {0};
  foreach (reduction(max:umax))
    if (norm(u) > umax)
      umax = norm(u);
  foreach()
    if (norm(u) == umax)
      pmax = (coord){x, y, z};
  fprintf (stderr, "umax 
}
~~~

Then look at what is in that cell: its volume fraction, its solid fraction,
its level, whether it is next to a boundary. The location usually points to
one of the causes above: an inflow in the wrong fluid, a tiny cut cell, a
ghost value of $f$ outside $[0,1]$.

## Testing

Test every change at a low level and short end time first: it shows within
minutes whether the program compiles, runs and keeps a healthy time step.
Such a run only shows that the change is stable. Whether it is right (a
reflection coefficient, a force, a convergence rate) needs a run at the
resolution and duration of the real case, or a 2D version of the problem,
which is often 100 times cheaper and enough to tune parameters.

